-- title:
--   Rollout improves a sequentially improving heuristic (Prop. 6.4.2)
-- statement:
--   **Proposition 6.4.2 (rollout improves on a sequentially improving heuristic).** Let the base heuristic $\mathcal{H}$ be sequentially improving, i.e. $\min_{j \in N(i)} H(j) \le H(i)$ at every non-destination node $i$, and let $(i_1, \dots, i_{\bar m})$ be a path generated by a terminating run of the rollout algorithm $R\mathcal{H}$ from a non-destination node $i_1$ to a destination $i_{\bar m}$. Then the rollout cost improves on the heuristic,
--
--   $$g(i_{\bar m}) \;\le\; H(i_1),$$
--
--   and is in fact the least of the heuristic cost at the start and at every neighbor visited along the way (Eq. (6.37)):
--
--   $$g(i_{\bar m}) \;=\; \min\Bigl\{\, H(i_1), \; \min_{j \in N(i_1)} H(j), \; \dots, \; \min_{j \in N(i_{\bar m - 1})} H(j) \,\Bigr\}.$$
--
--   This is the "rollout never hurts" theorem — the formal warrant for policy improvement by simulation, and the ancestor of the improvement step in Monte-Carlo tree search and modern reinforcement learning. The second identity is stronger than the first: it says the rollout has an *automatic cost sorting* property, always ending at the best destination among all those its base heuristic proposed along the run, so it can exhibit good behavior that the base heuristic alone never would.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The equality is phrased as a least element (`IsLeast`) of the displayed set, which asserts both that $g(i_{\bar m})$ is a lower bound and that it is attained by one of the listed values. Ties in the neighbor selection are permitted; the run is any complete node sequence satisfying the rollout selection rule.
-- source:
--   D. P. Bertsekas, Dynamic Programming and Optimal Control, Vol. I, 3rd ed., Athena Scientific, 2005, Proposition 6.4.2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BertsekasGraphSearch

namespace BertsekasDP

theorem rollout_sequential_improvement {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]
    (G : BertsekasGraphSearch V) (H : BertsekasBaseHeuristic G)
    (himp : BertsekasSeqImproving G H)
    (l : List V) (hrun : BertsekasIsRolloutRun G H l)
    (i₁ : V) (hhead : l.head? = some i₁) (hstart : i₁ ∉ G.dest)
    (d : V) (hlast : l.getLast? = some d) :
    G.cost d ≤ BertsekasHeurCost G H i₁ ∧
    IsLeast {c : ℝ | c = BertsekasHeurCost G H i₁ ∨
        ∃ v ∈ l.dropLast, ∃ j ∈ BertsekasNbrs G v, c = BertsekasHeurCost G H j}
      (G.cost d) := by sorry

end BertsekasDP
